.TH CVECTOR_APPEND_ARRAY 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cvector_append_array \- append an array of elements to a vector
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

bool cvector_append_array(cvec v, const void *arr_ptr, size_t elem_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function appends an array of elements to the end of the vector. It
reserves more capacity automatically when the vector needs space for all
the elements.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v           Vector to append to
arr_ptr     Pointer to the array of elements. The function only reads
            through it, so it can point at a const array.
elem_count  Number of elements in the array
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Gives true on success
Gives false when:
  - the new total count overflows or is more than ccol_max_elem_count
  - the memory allocation fails
  - arr_ptr aliases the backing buffer of v and elem_count reads past
    the end of the reservation of that buffer
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function appends all the elements or none of them.
The function reserves more capacity automatically when this is
necessary.
The function copies the elements into the vector.
The function asserts when v is NULL.
arr_ptr can safely point into the backing buffer of v. An example is a
pointer from cvector_data_ptr(3) or from cvector_at(3). A capacity
growth that this call starts does not invalidate such a pointer.
The source range and the destination range can overlap. One example is
an arr_ptr that aliases the buffer of v. Its elem_count then reads past
the current element count of the vector, into capacity that the vector
reserved already. The copy is safe when the ranges overlap.
A source range that aliases must stay inside the reserved capacity of v.
The call gives false, and leaves v unchanged, in one case. That case is
an arr_ptr that points into the backing buffer of v. Its elem_count then
reads past the end of the reservation of that buffer. Only a source range that aliases can have
this bound. An array of the caller has no extent that this function can
see. This is true for every C array that a caller passes as a bare
pointer.
The cvec_append_array(3) macro evaluates elem_count exactly once, and it
does this on the failure path too. An expression with a side effect is
therefore safe to pass.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_append_array (3),
.BR cvector_append_cvector (3)
